{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543752,"nonce":"3Wbk3IASgyMPzgwlKy8U6LkzD30=","target":"c68e3345bd7d1e72dc2d85ffb39d0c4ebf062fd2556437f7de4dfb262f5a53f1","message":"An axisymmetric Euler gas at rest returned NaN from `jno.solve.erk(\"ssprk3\", cfl=0.5)`, with HLL and with Rusanov, although its residual at the rest state is zero (1e-18). Expected: the gas stays at rest. Implicit marches failed the same way.\n\n**Reproduce** Euler in an (r, z) meridian written `r*q.t + (r*F_r).x + (r*F_z).y - (0, p, 0, 0)`, slip walls everywhere (the axis included), initial state at rest, marched with `jno.solve.erk(\"ssprk3\", cfl=0.5)`. The coupling test marches that gas, loaded on a wall, in `tests/test_coupling_stress.py::test_an_axisymmetric_gas_presses_on_an_axisymmetric_wall`. A first suspicion fell on the CFL estimate (zero wave speed weighted by r at the axis) or on the r = 0 faces; both were wrong. Until the fix the coupling test used the implicit default instead.\n\n**Cause** The probe that reads the `u.t` coefficient evaluated the whole volume expression at the constant state 0. There the axisymmetric source `p`, which contains `m/rho`, is 0/0, so the coefficient and with it the mass were NaN.\n\n**Fix** The probe now reads only the terms that hold a time derivative, never the source. The test above guards it: a gas at rest in the bore of a cylinder presses on an axisymmetric wall, compared with the Lame solution `u_r = (1+nu) p a^2 / (E (b^2-a^2)) [(1-2nu) r + b^2/r]`, and it marches with `erk(\"ssprk3\", cfl=0.5)`.","files":null}]}